"Bounds a business process by linking its suppliers and inputs to major activities, outputs and receiving customers.\n\nUse when: Agree the scope before detailed process mapping. Clarify what a process must receive and deliver.\n\nAvoid when: Detailed branch order, task ownership or exception handling is required.\n\nVisual grammar: Five labeled columns distinguish suppliers, inputs, process, outputs and customers. A short activity sequence occupies the process column. Optional constraints and measures remain clearly identified supplements.\n\nBuild: Define the process trigger, end point and intended customer. List a small number of major activities and observable outputs. Trace outputs to customers and inputs back to supplying sources.\n\nCheck: Suppliers provide named inputs needed by the process. Each output has a recipient and acceptance expectation. Process boundaries agree with the stated analysis scope.\n\nPitfalls: Listing departments as outputs instead of deliverables. Turning the overview into dozens of detailed procedural steps.\n\nExample: For equipment repair, identify the requester, faulty device, assess-repair-test activities, restored equipment and receiving operator.\n\nAlternatives: Process classification hierarchy, Value-stream map."
